Render Arabic announcements right-to-left

Direction is decided per announcement item, not per page, since an admin will
mix English and Arabic entries in the same table. English is unchanged.

- Each <article> gets dir="rtl" or "ltr" from its own text. RTL mirrors the
  layout: text right-aligned, "Learn more" returns to the far left, and the
  arrow flips with scaleX(-1) so it points the way the reader travels. The RTL
  hover nudge is scaleX(-1) translateX(3px) so it keeps sliding with the
  reading direction.
- Direction comes from the FIRST strongly-scripted character of the title, with
  the body as tie-break, matching how a browser resolves dir="auto". Digits and
  punctuation are skipped, so a bare "2026" or a URL stays LTR.
- The admin live preview mirrors this too, so typing Arabic in the form shows
  the real right-aligned result.
- Adds tests/announcements.test.ts: 5 tests covering Arabic titles, title
  taking precedence over an Arabic body, ambiguous input staying LTR, the
  javascript:/data: rejection in announcementHref, and the date stamp. Unit
  suite 13/13, up from 8. These caught a real bug in the first attempt, which
  flipped an English headline merely quoting Arabic in its body.

Verified: 13/13 unit, typecheck, eslint incl. components/lib, static build,
browser check of English/Arabic/no-link cases side by side, 158/158 QA, 33/33
motion, 21/21 overflow.

Not addressed: the site only loads Latin-subset webfonts, so Arabic renders in
the OS fallback rather than the site's serif.
